{"title":"Pascal's Theorem","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><table style=\"border:thin solid black;padding:5px;float:right;margin:10px;\">\n\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"border:thin solid gray;\">\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/d/91dbf863f1aac9ed172e64dbbb565045bbff013b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] size(250); D(unitcircle,red); pair A=dir(20), B=dir(40),       C=dir(70), D=dir(120),      E=dir(210),F=dir(300); D(L(D(IP(D(L(A,B,0.5,7)),D(L(E,D,0.5,1.5)))), D(IP(D(L(C,B,0.5,10)),D(L(E,F,0.5,3.3)))),0.1),dashed); D(IP(D(L(A,F,1,0.5)),D(L(C,D,2,0.5))));  D(A--B--C--D--E--F--A,black+linewidth(1));       [/asy]\" width=\"418\" height=\"335\" />\t\n</p>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td><div class=\"magnify\" style=\"float: right;\">\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-right\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Enlarge.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/5/58/Enlarge.png/15px-Enlarge.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"15\" height=\"9\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/5/58/Enlarge.png/23px-Enlarge.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/5/58/Enlarge.png/30px-Enlarge.png 2x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure></div> A diagram of the theorem\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><b>Pascal's Theorem</b> is a result in <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Projective_geometry\" title=\"Projective geometry\">projective geometry</a>.  It states that if a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Hexagon\" title=\"Hexagon\">hexagon</a> is inscribed in a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Conic_section\" title=\"Conic section\">conic section</a>, then the points of intersection of the pairs of its opposite sides are collinear:\n</p><p>Since it is a result in the projective plane, it has a dual, <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Brianchon%27s_Theorem\" title=\"Brianchon&#39;s Theorem\">Brianchon's Theorem</a>, which states that the diagonals of a hexagon circumscribed about a conic concur.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proof\">Proof</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Pascal%27s_Theorem&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Proof\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>It is sufficient to prove the result for a hexagon inscribed in a circle, for <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Affine_transformations&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Affine transformations (page does not exist)\">affine transformations</a> map this circle to any ellipse while preserving collinearity and concurrence in the projective plane, and projective transformations can map an ellipse to any conic while similarly preserving collinearity and concurrence in the projective sense.  Thus we will prove the theorem for a cyclic hexagon, using directed angles mod <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/c/f2ca003a7da0de4994b4733e203b74ff52d42553.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pi$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" />.\n</p><p><b>Lemma.</b> Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/8/178ef0c25cb393195368ae91e70341404562e677.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega_1, \\omega_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"44\" height=\"11\" /> be two circles which intersect at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/0/ec00004a5113b974f70a8e26c3781261e56464e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M, N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"43\" height=\"16\" />, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> be a chord of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/d/cddbccf89c00e019e9878f87f8d6325697e3f2fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"13\" />, and let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/7/1375da8b94d688b4928311f04d35a57081ee0f1e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C, D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"36\" height=\"16\" /> be the second intersections of lines <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/f/b7f4f0c4d07a35bf1c8156f36bf567c0dc2d368a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AM, BN$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"71\" height=\"16\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/e/7cef5fa7ec053472a6e471aaacb0c5927ce36b36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"13\" />.  Then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/4/0948661e822f2a953c43a57ac9e40b2734476de4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> are parallel.\n</p><p><i>Proof.</i>  Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/8/2989a36f2f820adde8d7a51b5f5f2d641e068856.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABNM, CDMN$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"137\" height=\"16\" /> are two sets of concyclic points and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/c/49c135a91dbf6da5d71d4499f0f897437ca5f274.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A,M,C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"62\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/e/51ecf559d8139afccc8f04d8b53aea3854487258.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B,N,D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"61\" height=\"16\" /> are two sets of collinear points,\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/3/953f24d84e084a3d5d2038161b50ae18d0fd5e50.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle CAB \\equiv \\angle MAB \\equiv 180^\\circ -\\angle MNB \\equiv \\angle MND \\equiv 180^\\circ - \\angle MCD \\equiv 180^\\circ - \\angle ACD$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"655\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>Because alternate interior angles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/9/459b39e026037707282c95dfdbfd6dc64c855a16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CAB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/e/6ce2cd123db24a7b8373999aea7a98927e3b3c81.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ACD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"45\" height=\"14\" /> are congruent, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/b/c9b7a9ce12fab080c5a4186e6f6807fcdd4b0cda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB || CD.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"72\" height=\"18\" /><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Template:Halmos&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Template:Halmos (page does not exist)\">Template:Halmos</a>\n</p><p><b>Theorem.</b> Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/8/f78a281bf85c5b37b6f63bff824abe1169705529.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1A_2A_3A_4A_5A_6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"125\" height=\"15\" /> be a cyclic hexagon, and let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/7/6670e48706eeb2b0052e60849af08af18eb3910e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_1 = A_1A_2 \\cap A_4A_5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"146\" height=\"15\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/3/753cf428386968a76b2f66c13e24890d08b07c41.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_2 = A_2A_3 \\cap A_5A_6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"146\" height=\"15\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/8/e4852b25b153e0fb7ff58b4f6398a751900923d4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_3 = A_3A_4 \\cap A_6A_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"146\" height=\"15\" />.  Then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/d/3fd628c2338dc339d3e6a10257752d208924b1eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_1, P_2, P_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"72\" height=\"16\" /> are collinear.\n</p><p><i>Proof.</i> Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/d/cddbccf89c00e019e9878f87f8d6325697e3f2fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"13\" /> be the circumcircle of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/8/f78a281bf85c5b37b6f63bff824abe1169705529.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1A_2A_3A_4A_5A_6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"125\" height=\"15\" />, and let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/e/7cef5fa7ec053472a6e471aaacb0c5927ce36b36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"13\" /> be the circumcircle of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/9/b99aa56f05d8e809cc44186f6c7bbba8fde0f15a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle{A_2A_5P_2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"76\" height=\"16\" />. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/3/243af6fad28c15abb3e11996374ba60e745bf543.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"23\" height=\"17\" /> be the second intersection of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/e/7cef5fa7ec053472a6e471aaacb0c5927ce36b36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"13\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/1/ef100258eefc3cb01f88675aaf511c44213fbf8c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1A_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"40\" height=\"15\" />, and let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/8/368b6f971b418d731c62ff2cb59f51c527d22849.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"23\" height=\"17\" /> be the second intersection of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/d/21d9ed67d3a56bf106a98b80257b58ed47ee115b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_4A_5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"40\" height=\"15\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/e/7cef5fa7ec053472a6e471aaacb0c5927ce36b36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"13\" />. By lemma, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/0/380d77440156a62ebb0cb962115b3f4ec9a6693c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1P_3 \\parallel B_1P_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"98\" height=\"19\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/f/95f61bc930542643dea70d6c55131d4f04ccb473.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1A_4 \\parallel B_1B_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"102\" height=\"19\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/6/c166efa073494833d4fea45f2295608b1bd833eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_3A_4 \\parallel P_2B_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"98\" height=\"19\" />, see figure. It follows that triangles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/0/9/209d84e98760f51de49b5328503b8b275add01a9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle{P_3A_1A_4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"76\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/9/4d9b7f8e29d13fab1c8a5404087aa2535c1de59b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle{P_2B_1B_2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"76\" height=\"16\" /> are homothetic with a center <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/b/a5b0256a78d58b6d5f7a53400b19e7747d2983fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"17\" />, because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/2/5f28049bb9d1ce18f3107c5773c90cb00831f92b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1B_1 \\cap A_4B_2 = P_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"146\" height=\"15\" />. Therefore the line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/e/6eeff8aba6714e6d5afe91b66a31c6774705a9ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_3P_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"36\" height=\"15\" /> passes through the center of homothety, i.e. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/b/a5b0256a78d58b6d5f7a53400b19e7747d2983fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"17\" />.  <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Template:Halmos&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Template:Halmos (page does not exist)\">Template:Halmos</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Notes\">Notes</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Pascal%27s_Theorem&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Notes\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>In our proof, we never assumed anything about configuration.  Thus the hexagon need not even be convex for the theorem to hold.  In fact, many useful applications of the theorem occur with degenerate hexagons, i.e., hexagons in which not all of the points are distinct.  In the case that two points are the same, we consider the line through them to be the tangent to the conic through that point.  For instance, when we let a triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> be a \"hexagon\" <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/6/ff6063a196fea54a7ce25b43bab33da0a9c9f2ce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AABBCC$\" width=\"85\" height=\"13\" />, Pascal's Theorem tells us that if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/0/da0540c5113aa24d00cc5b5674039cab66098a34.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\ell_A, \\ell_B, \\ell_C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"71\" height=\"16\" /> are the tangents to the circumcircle of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> that pass through <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/8/a9874ea56cf8fec1bd246793224fbcfaad17ab3f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A,B,C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" />, respectively, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/d/02d30b26411a5d0ca19db85082fb198f811587cf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\ell_A \\cap BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"67\" height=\"15\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/d/e1d10d20c60295a8499bd166e4630c8fdbfc7de8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\ell_B \\cap CA$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"67\" height=\"15\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/0/8d0fce6627c387493f60298396031eff8c9caba7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\ell_C \\cap AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"66\" height=\"15\" /> are collinear; the line they determine is called the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Lemoine_Axis&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Lemoine Axis (page does not exist)\">Lemoine Axis</a>.  In fact, Pascal's Theorem tells us that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/0/da0540c5113aa24d00cc5b5674039cab66098a34.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\ell_A, \\ell_B, \\ell_C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"71\" height=\"16\" /> can be the tangent lines to any conic circumscribed about triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> and the result still holds.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Small_Pascal.27s_theorem\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Small_Pascal's_theorem\">Small Pascal's theorem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Pascal%27s_Theorem&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Small Pascal&#39;s theorem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> and point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> be given. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/2/9e2b196e9b7e57d1ec99f6534c581ea9759d2170.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Omega$\" width=\"12\" height=\"13\" /> be the circumcircle of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/6/a06b6a76d94015f6409a7a30269e0079f66826cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC, A&#39; = AP \\cap \\Omega, B&#39; = BP \\cap \\Omega, C&#39; = CP \\cap \\Omega.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"403\" height=\"19\" />\nLet the tangent line to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/2/9e2b196e9b7e57d1ec99f6534c581ea9759d2170.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Omega$\" width=\"12\" height=\"13\" /> at point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> cross line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/e/6aeaf3b3ed66bc4256b58d8123fbf178f31df9a7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B&#39;C&#39;$\" width=\"37\" height=\"14\" /> at point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/1/c61f1e8be9d73f590167b39417e42bbaa1787fe0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D.$\" width=\"19\" height=\"12\" /> Similarly denote points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/9/f2920fa2bc40ed68149a854f8b32c09f619b0d70.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F.$\" width=\"15\" height=\"12\" />\nProve that the points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/0/87082784a051c7525966d9819cc379329a30e030.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D, E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"37\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> are collinear. \n</p><p><i><b>Proof</b></i>\n</p><p><i><b>1. Solution in Barycentric coordinates.</b></i>\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Barycentric_coordinates\" title=\"Barycentric coordinates\"> Small Pascal's theorem</a>\n</p><p><i><b>2. Simplest case -  Lemoine point.</b></i>\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> be the Lemoine point.\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018102744\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.039 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.075 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 327/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 307/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 60/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 11971/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%   21.244      1 -total\n 52.21%   11.092      1 Template:Asy_image\n 14.03%    2.980      2 Template:Halmos\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:4992:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018102744 and revision id 221786. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}